From my own knowledge: The classic motor starter rung requires a Start button (normally closed, so it acts as an XIC) and a Stop button (normally open, so it acts as an XIO). The overload contact is a normally closed safety device (XIC). The seal-in is the motor's own contact, which is normally open (XIC) and must be in series with the Start button to prevent the motor from running if the Start button is released.

Here is the ladder logic:

```text
// Start button (NO) OR Motor's own contact (XIC)
// This creates the seal-in. If the motor is running, its contact is closed.
// If the Start button is released, the seal-in opens and the motor stops.
XIC(StartPB)XIC(Motor)XIC(OverloadOK)XIC(StopPB)OTE(Motor);
// Run light follows the motor
XIC(Motor)OTE(RunLight);
```

